---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import joshPorchImg from '../assets/images/headshots/josh-porch.jpg';
import joshPerformingImg from '../assets/images/josh-performing.jpg';
---

<Layout title="Speaking | Josh Turknett, MD" currentPage="speaking" description="Keynotes and workshops on learning, performance under pressure, and the brain, from neurologist Josh Turknett, author of The Genius and the Impostor. Tailored to your audience, with live music.">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container page-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Speaking</span>
        <h1 class="page-hero-title">Keynotes and workshops on learning, performance, and the brain</h1>
        <p class="hero-subtitle">I'm a neurologist and the author of <em>The Genius and the Impostor</em>. My keynotes combine the science of learning and performance with live banjo.</p>
        <a href="#inquire" class="cta">Check availability</a>
      </div>
      <div class="page-hero-photo">
        <Image src={joshPorchImg} width={900} widths={[480, 720, 900]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 90vw, 40vw" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" alt="Josh Turknett" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Who I speak to -->
  <section class="section section--gray">
    <div class="container narrow fade-in">
      <h2>Who I speak to</h2>
      <p class="lead">I've given talks at business and HR events, research institutes, medical conferences, learning and development gatherings, and music camps. I've also worked one-on-one with musicians and athletes on performing under pressure.</p>
      <p>Each of those audiences comes in with its own version of two questions. Why do capable people fall short when it counts? And where does ability come from in the first place? A leadership team wants to know why their best people freeze in the big meeting. A room full of teachers wants to know what learning science says about the kid who "isn't a math person." A group of musicians wants to know why the piece they played perfectly at home falls apart on stage. The same neuroscience answers all three. So before every talk, I find out what your audience is asking, and I build the talk around it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Signature talks -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="fade-in">Signature talks</h2>

      <article class="talk fade-in" id="genius-impostor">
        <h3>The Genius and the Impostor</h3>
        <p class="talk-sub">Why Skilled People Underperform When It Matters Most</p>
        <p>You've seen it happen. Someone who knows their stuff cold walks into the high-stakes moment and delivers half of what they're capable of. The skills didn't go anywhere. So what happened?</p>
        <p>The skills you've mastered run on non-conscious expert networks. They were built by the part of your brain I call the Genius, and they work best when the conscious mind stays out of the way. When the stakes go up, you feel a rush of nerves. That rush is your brain getting ready to perform at its best. The trouble starts with how the voice in your head, which I call the Impostor, responds to it. He turns the moment into a test of who you are and grabs the controls from the Genius. But the Impostor lives in the conscious mind, and the conscious mind is a novice. It doesn't know how to run those skills. That's why you can feel like a beginner in those moments.</p>
        <p>In this talk, I show audiences what's happening in their brains in those moments, and what they can do to keep their skills working when the stakes are high.</p>
        <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit for:</strong> leadership and corporate teams, sales and client-facing groups, athletes and coaches, musicians and performers.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="talk fade-in" id="special-brain">
        <h3>The Special Brain Story</h3>
        <p class="talk-sub">What Neuroscience Says About Talent</p>
        <p>Most of us grew up with some version of the same story. I call it the Special Brain Story. Some people are born with a gift for math, or music, or sports, and the rest of us aren't. Schools sort kids by it. Companies hire by it. And most of us decided long ago which things we're "just not good at."</p>
        <p>Neuroscience tells a different story. Every brain starts in the same place, and every skill, including the ones that look like genius, is built through learning. The differences between people come from what they've built.</p>
        <p>This talk walks through that science and what it means for how we teach, train, hire, and lead.</p>
        <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit for:</strong> educators, HR and talent development, learning and development teams, general audiences.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Live music -->
  <section class="section section--gray">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split">
        <div class="split-image fade-in">
          <Image src={joshPerformingImg} width={1200} widths={[600, 900, 1200]} sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Josh Turknett playing banjo on stage" />
        </div>
        <div class="split-content fade-in fade-in-delay-1">
          <h2>With live music</h2>
          <p class="lead">Between sections, I pick up the banjo and play. The brain learns best in short bursts of focus followed by rest, and the music gives the audience that rest.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- More topics -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow fade-in">
      <h2>More topics</h2>
      <p>I also speak on these, and I'm happy to build a talk around your event's theme.</p>
      <ul class="topic-list">
        <li><strong>The Neuroscience of Practice.</strong> Why some people practice for years and plateau, while others improve faster with less time.</li>
        <li><strong>Training That Sticks.</strong> What the brain needs to hold on to new learning and use it on the job, and how to design training around it.</li>
        <li><strong>The Neuroscience of Coaching.</strong> Why some of the most common things coaches say make performance worse, and what to say instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Performance Neurology</strong> (for medical audiences). What happens when neurology's diagnostic methods are aimed at why capable people fail to perform.</li>
      </ul>
      <a href="https://performanceneurology.institute/speaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="small-link">See the full list at the Institute for Performance Neurology →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Formats -->
  <section class="section section--gray">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="fade-in">Formats</h2>
      <div class="preview-grid fade-in fade-in-delay-1">
        <div class="preview-card">
          <h3>Keynote</h3>
          <p>45 to 60 minutes of science and live music. Works for conferences, all-hands meetings, and kickoffs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="preview-card">
          <h3>Workshop</h3>
          <p>A half or full day, with more time on the science and exercises your group works through together. Best for leadership teams and groups who want to put the ideas to work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="preview-card">
          <h3>Extended program</h3>
          <p>Several sessions over multiple days, with talks, workshops, and music throughout. Best for retreats.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Inquiry form (Netlify Forms) -->
  <section class="section" id="inquire">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>Tell me about your event</h2>
      <form name="speaking-inquiry" method="POST" action="/thanks" data-netlify="true" netlify-honeypot="bot-field" class="inquiry-form">
        <input type="hidden" name="form-name" value="speaking-inquiry" />
        <p class="hidden-field"><label>Leave this empty: <input name="bot-field" /></label></p>
        <div class="form-row">
          <div class="form-group"><label for="f-name">Name</label><input id="f-name" type="text" name="name" required autocomplete="name" /></div>
          <div class="form-group"><label for="f-email">Email</label><input id="f-email" type="email" name="email" required autocomplete="email" /></div>
        </div>
        <div class="form-row">
          <div class="form-group"><label for="f-org">Organization</label><input id="f-org" type="text" name="organization" autocomplete="organization" /></div>
          <div class="form-group"><label for="f-date">Event date</label><input id="f-date" type="text" name="event-date" placeholder="Date or rough timeframe" /></div>
        </div>
        <div class="form-row">
          <div class="form-group"><label for="f-loc">Location</label><input id="f-loc" type="text" name="location" placeholder="City, or virtual" /></div>
          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="f-format">Format</label>
            <select id="f-format" name="format">
              <option>Keynote</option>
              <option>Workshop</option>
              <option>Extended program</option>
              <option>Not sure yet</option>
            </select>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="form-group"><label for="f-aud">Audience</label><input id="f-aud" type="text" name="audience" placeholder="Who they are, and roughly how many" /></div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="f-budget">Budget range</label>
          <select id="f-budget" name="budget">
            <option>Under $5k</option>
            <option>$5k-10k</option>
            <option>$10k-20k</option>
            <option>$20k+</option>
            <option selected>Not sure yet</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="form-group"><label for="f-msg">Anything else I should know</label><textarea id="f-msg" name="message"></textarea></div>
        <button type="submit">Send</button>
        <p class="form-note">I'll get back to you within a few days.</p>
      </form>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
